(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-05"] = {"chem-11-05-b01":{"idea":"The s-block has two groups. Group 1 holds the alkali metals, and you must know which element sits where.","steps":["Group 1 elements are Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs and Fr.","Sodium (Na) is in this list.","Magnesium and calcium are in Group 2.","Aluminium is in Group 13, the boron group."],"others":"Magnesium and Calcium are Group 2 metals. Aluminium is a p-block metal in Group 13.","trap":"Remember the order Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr as the alkali metals.","answer":"Sodium"},"chem-11-05-b02":{"idea":"The number of valence electrons decides the usual charge of an ion.","steps":["Group 2 atoms have two electrons in the outer s orbital (ns²).","They lose both electrons easily to reach a noble-gas shell.","So the ion formed is M²⁺, and the oxidation state is +2."],"others":"+1 is the Group 1 state, where only one electron is lost. +3 needs a third electron removed from an inner shell, which costs too much energy. Variable states belong to transition metals.","answer":"+2"},"chem-11-05-b03":{"idea":"Many everyday chemicals have common names. NaHCO₃ is the one used in cooking.","steps":["NaHCO₃ is sodium hydrogencarbonate (sodium bicarbonate).","It is used in baking because it releases CO₂ on heating, which makes dough rise.","So its common name is baking soda."],"others":"Slaked lime is Ca(OH)₂. Caustic soda is NaOH. Washing soda is Na₂CO₃·10H₂O.","trap":"Baking soda has one Na and an H. Washing soda has two Na.","answer":"Baking soda"},"chem-11-05-b04":{"idea":"Hydrated sodium carbonate is used for washing clothes and softening water.","steps":["Na₂CO₃·10H₂O is sodium carbonate with 10 molecules of water of crystallisation.","This crystalline form is sold for cleaning clothes.","Its common name is washing soda."],"others":"Quicklime is CaO. Epsom salt is MgSO₄·7H₂O. Baking soda is NaHCO₃, which has no water of crystallisation.","answer":"Washing soda"},"chem-11-05-b05":{"idea":"Calcium oxide has a special common name linked to its reaction with water.","steps":["CaO is calcium oxide.","It is made by heating limestone.","Its common name is quicklime."],"others":"Plaster of Paris is CaSO₄·½H₂O. Slaked lime is Ca(OH)₂, which forms when quicklime meets water. Limestone is CaCO₃.","trap":"Quicklime is CaO, slaked lime is Ca(OH)₂. Do not swap them.","answer":"Quicklime"},"chem-11-05-b06":{"idea":"Calcium hydroxide is made when quicklime reacts with water.","steps":["Ca(OH)₂ is calcium hydroxide.","It forms from CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂, which is called slaking.","So its common name is slaked lime."],"others":"Quicklime is CaO, before slaking. Gypsum is CaSO₄·2H₂O. Limestone is CaCO₃.","answer":"Slaked lime"},"chem-11-05-b07":{"idea":"Metal ions give a coloured flame because excited electrons release light of a fixed colour.","steps":["In a flame test, the salt is heated in a flame.","Each metal gives its own colour.","Sodium gives a strong golden-yellow flame."],"others":"Caesium gives a blue colour. Lithium gives crimson red. Potassium gives a lilac (pale violet) colour.","trap":"Sodium is yellow, potassium is lilac, lithium is crimson.","answer":"Sodium"},"chem-11-05-b08":{"idea":"Flame colours help identify s-block metals.","steps":["Lithium salts give a crimson-red flame.","Potassium gives lilac and sodium gives golden-yellow.","Magnesium gives no characteristic colour."],"others":"Potassium shows lilac. Sodium shows golden-yellow. Magnesium shows no colour because its electrons need more energy than the flame gives.","answer":"Lithium"},"chem-11-05-b09":{"idea":"Alkali metals have only one valence electron per atom, so their metallic bonds are weak.","steps":["Each atom gives just one electron to the metallic bond.","Their atoms are large, so the bond is weak.","Weak bonds make the metals soft. Sodium can be cut with a knife."],"others":"They are metals, not brittle non-metals. They are not hard like transition metals. They are solids at room temperature, so liquid is wrong.","answer":"Soft, and can be cut with a knife"},"chem-11-05-b10":{"idea":"Calcium carbonate occurs naturally in rocks.","steps":["CaCO₃ is calcium carbonate.","It is found in nature as limestone, marble and chalk.","So its common name is limestone."],"others":"Quicklime is CaO. Baking soda is NaHCO₃. Slaked lime is Ca(OH)₂.","answer":"Limestone"},"chem-11-05-b11":{"idea":"Know which elements fall in Group 2, the alkaline earth metals.","steps":["Group 2 is Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba and Ra.","Calcium is in this list.","Potassium and sodium are Group 1. Aluminium is Group 13."],"others":"Potassium and Sodium are alkali metals of Group 1. Aluminium belongs to Group 13.","answer":"Calcium"},"chem-11-05-b12":{"idea":"Each period down a group adds one more electron shell.","steps":["Going down Group 1 from Li to Cs, one new shell is added each time.","The outer electron sits farther from the nucleus.","So the atomic radius increases steadily."],"others":"It does not decrease, because shells are added. It does not stay constant. It does not rise then fall, because the trend down a group is regular.","trap":"Radius falls across a period but rises down a group.","answer":"It increases"},"chem-11-05-b13":{"idea":"Ionisation energy is the energy needed to remove the outer electron.","steps":["Down Group 1, the atom gets bigger.","The outer electron is farther from the nucleus and is shielded by more inner shells.","It is easier to remove, so ionisation energy decreases."],"others":"It does not increase, since the electron is held more loosely. It does not stay constant. It does not change direction, since the trend is regular.","answer":"It decreases"},"chem-11-05-b14":{"idea":"Molar mass is found by adding the atomic masses of all atoms in the formula.","steps":["CaCO₃ has 1 Ca, 1 C and 3 O.","M = 40 + 12 + 3 × 16.","M = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100 g/mol."],"trap":"Do not forget the 3 before O.","answer":"100 g/mol"},"chem-11-05-b15":{"idea":"Mass percentage of an element = (mass of element in one mole ÷ molar mass) × 100.","steps":["In one mole of CaCO₃ there is 40 g of Ca.","Molar mass of CaCO₃ is 100 g/mol.","Mass % of Ca = (40 ÷ 100) × 100 = 40%.","Check: C and O together are 60 g, which is 60%, and 40% + 60% = 100%."],"answer":"40%"},"chem-11-05-b16":{"idea":"A diagonal relationship means an element resembles the one diagonally below-right in the next period.","steps":["Li (Period 2, Group 1) resembles Mg (Period 3, Group 2).","Be (Period 2, Group 2) resembles Al (Period 3, Group 13).","The reason is a similar charge-to-size ratio."],"others":"The statement about equal valence electrons is wrong, since Be and Al differ. Li and Na are in the same group, so that is not diagonal. Radii are not equal on a diagonal.","answer":"Some Period 2 elements resemble the Period 3 element diagonally below-right, e.g. Li and Mg"},"chem-11-05-b17":{"idea":"Li and Mg are similar. You must find the statement that is false.","steps":["Li₂CO₃ and MgCO₃ both break down on heating to give the oxide and CO₂.","So saying both carbonates are stable to heat is false.","The question asks for the NOT similarity, so this is the answer."],"others":"The other three statements are true similarities. Both form covalent organometallic compounds, both carbonates decompose, and both react with N₂ to give nitrides.","trap":"Read the word NOT before you choose.","answer":"Both form stable carbonates that do not decompose on heating"},"chem-11-05-b18":{"idea":"Alkali halides are mostly soluble. LiF is the exception.","steps":["LiF has very small Li⁺ and F⁻ ions.","Small ions attract strongly, so the lattice enthalpy is very high.","Water cannot overcome it, so LiF is poorly soluble."],"others":"NaCl, KCl and NaF are all freely soluble in water.","answer":"LiF"},"chem-11-05-b19":{"idea":"Solubility of a salt depends on lattice enthalpy and hydration enthalpy.","steps":["Down Group 2, the cation gets larger.","Hydration enthalpy falls faster than lattice enthalpy for sulfates.","So BeSO₄ and MgSO₄ dissolve, CaSO₄ is slightly soluble and BaSO₄ is almost insoluble.","Sulfate solubility therefore decreases down the group."],"others":"The other choices say it stays the same, rises, or goes down then up. BaSO₄ is a well-known insoluble white solid, so these are wrong.","answer":"Decreases: BeSO4 and MgSO4 dissolve; BaSO4 is almost insoluble"},"chem-11-05-b20":{"idea":"Hydroxides follow the opposite trend to sulfates in Group 2.","steps":["Mg(OH)₂ is almost insoluble in water.","Ba(OH)₂ is fairly soluble.","So hydroxide solubility increases down Group 2."],"others":"Decreases is the sulfate trend, not the hydroxide trend. All hydroxides are not nearly insoluble, since Ba(OH)₂ dissolves well. Ca(OH)₂ is less soluble than Ba(OH)₂, so it is not a peak.","trap":"Sulfates fall, hydroxides rise down Group 2.","answer":"Increases: Mg(OH)2 is nearly insoluble; Ba(OH)2 is fairly soluble"},"chem-11-05-b21":{"idea":"Small cations distort (polarise) the carbonate ion and help it break down into oxide and CO₂.","steps":["Going down a group the cation gets bigger.","A big cation has lower charge density and polarises CO₃²⁻ less.","The carbonate is distorted less, so it needs more heat to decompose.","Thermal stability increases down both groups."],"others":"Larger cations hold the carbonate ion less tightly is wrong, since weaker polarisation means more stability. Different trends for the two groups is wrong. A single temperature for all is wrong.","answer":"Increases: larger cations polarise the carbonate ion less"},"chem-11-05-b22":{"idea":"Flame colour needs the outer electron to be excited by the flame's energy.","steps":["Na, K and Ca give sodium yellow, lilac and brick red.","Mg atoms are small and hold their electrons tightly.","The Bunsen flame has too little energy to excite them.","So magnesium gives no characteristic colour."],"others":"Sodium, Potassium and Calcium all give clear flame colours (yellow, lilac, brick red).","answer":"Magnesium"},"chem-11-05-b23":{"idea":"Be is the smallest Group 2 atom, so its ion has a very high charge density.","steps":["Be²⁺ is tiny and carries a 2+ charge.","This gives it high polarising power.","It pulls electron clouds of anions towards itself.","So Be compounds are largely covalent, unlike other Group 2 compounds."],"others":"Very large ionic size is wrong, since Be²⁺ is the smallest. Beryllium is a metal. Its oxidation state is +2, not +1.","answer":"Very small ionic size, giving high polarizing power and covalent character"},"chem-11-05-b24":{"idea":"The Solvay process is an industrial method for one sodium compound.","steps":["Brine (NaCl), ammonia and CO₂ are used.","NaHCO₃ is precipitated and then heated.","The final product is Na₂CO₃, called washing soda or soda ash."],"others":"KCl and MgSO₄ are not made this way. CaCO₃ is a raw material of the process (it gives CO₂), not its product.","answer":"Na2CO3 (washing soda)"},"chem-11-05-b26":{"idea":"Quicklime reacts with water in a strongly exothermic reaction called slaking.","steps":["Quicklime is CaO and slaked lime is Ca(OH)₂.","CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂.","Check that atoms balance: 1 Ca, 2 O and 2 H on both sides."],"others":"CaO + CO₂ → CaCO₃ uses carbon dioxide, not water. Ca(OH)₂ → CaO + H₂O is the reverse. CaCO₃ + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ does not happen.","answer":"CaO + H2O -> Ca(OH)2"},"chem-11-05-b27":{"idea":"Limestone gives quicklime and CO₂ when heated strongly. This is how lime is made.","steps":["Heat CaCO₃ at high temperature.","It breaks into CaO (solid) and CO₂ (gas).","CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂, and the atoms balance."],"others":"CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ is slaking. 2CaCO₃ → 2CaO + O₂ is wrong, since it gives O₂ and not CO₂. Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O is the hardening of lime wash.","answer":"CaCO3 -> CaO + CO2"},"chem-11-05-b28":{"idea":"Use mole ratios from a balanced equation to find the mass of a product.","steps":["Moles of CaCO₃ = 50 ÷ 100 = 0.5 mol.","The equation is 1 : 1, so CO₂ is also 0.5 mol.","Mass of CO₂ = 0.5 × 44 = 22.0 g.","Check: CaO is 0.5 × 56 = 28 g, and 28 + 22 = 50 g, the starting mass."],"answer":"22.0 g"},"chem-11-05-b29":{"idea":"Calcium hydroxide has a famous household use.","steps":["Slaked lime mixed with water is called lime wash.","It is painted on walls for whitewashing.","In air it absorbs CO₂ and turns into hard CaCO₃."],"others":"Slaked lime is not a reducing agent. The main part of cement clinker is calcium silicates, not Ca(OH)₂. It is a base, so it would not dry acidic gases; it would react with them.","answer":"Whitewashing walls (as lime wash)"},"chem-11-05-b30":{"idea":"A reducing agent gives away electrons.","steps":["Group 1 and Group 2 atoms are large.","They have only one or two valence electrons.","Their ionisation energies are low, so they lose electrons easily.","Losing electrons easily means being a strong reducing agent."],"others":"High electronegativity means pulling electrons in, which is the opposite. A full outer shell belongs to noble gases. These elements are metals, not non-metals.","answer":"Low ionization energy makes them lose electrons easily"},"chem-11-05-b31":{"idea":"Calcium has a key structural role in the body.","steps":["Bones and teeth are mostly calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite).","So the main s-block element there is calcium."],"others":"Magnesium, Sodium and Potassium are needed in the body but do not form the bulk of bones and teeth.","answer":"Calcium"},"chem-11-05-b32":{"idea":"Sodium and potassium ions are split between outside and inside of cells.","steps":["Sodium ions are mostly outside animal cells.","They control the osmotic balance of that fluid.","Potassium plays this role inside cells."],"others":"Potassium (K⁺) is mainly inside the cell. Magnesium (Mg²⁺) and Calcium (Ca²⁺) have other roles, such as enzymes, muscle action and bones.","trap":"Na⁺ outside, K⁺ inside.","answer":"Sodium (Na+)"},"chem-11-05-b33":{"idea":"The green pigment of plants has a metal atom at its centre.","steps":["Chlorophyll has Mg²⁺ at the centre of its ring.","Magnesium is an s-block element.","So magnesium is the central metal of chlorophyll."],"others":"Haemoglobin has iron. Vitamin B12 has cobalt. Insulin is a protein hormone with no central metal ion in its structure.","answer":"Chlorophyll"},"chem-11-05-b34":{"idea":"Plaster of Paris takes up water and sets into hard gypsum.","steps":["Plaster of Paris is CaSO₄·½H₂O. Gypsum is CaSO₄·2H₂O.","Setting adds water: 2CaSO₄·½H₂O + 3H₂O → 2CaSO₄·2H₂O.","Check water: left has 2 × ½ + 3 = 4, right has 2 × 2 = 4."],"others":"The reaction that gives hemihydrate from gypsum is the reverse, which is how plaster is made by heating. The other forms are unbalanced or use the wrong starting solid.","answer":"2CaSO4.0.5H2O + 3H2O -> 2CaSO4.2H2O"},"chem-11-05-b35":{"idea":"Add up the atomic masses for each atom in the formula.","steps":["CaSO₄ has 1 Ca, 1 S and 4 O.","M = 40 + 32 + 4 × 16.","M = 40 + 32 + 64 = 136 g/mol."],"answer":"136 g/mol"},"chem-11-05-b36":{"idea":"Percentage of water = (mass of water in one mole ÷ molar mass of the hydrate) × 100.","steps":["Gypsum CaSO₄·2H₂O has 2 × 18 = 36 g water per mole.","Its molar mass is 172 g/mol.","Mass % water = (36 ÷ 172) × 100 = 20.93%.","Check: CaSO₄ is 136 ÷ 172 = 79.07%, and 79.07 + 20.93 = 100."],"answer":"20.93%"},"chem-11-05-b37":{"idea":"The group number tells the valence shell configuration.","steps":["Group 1 atoms have one outer electron.","It sits in an s orbital of the outer shell n.","So the configuration is ns¹. For example Na is [Ne] 3s¹."],"others":"ns²np⁶ is for noble gases. ns²np¹ is Group 13. ns² is Group 2.","answer":"ns1"},"chem-11-05-b38":{"idea":"Alkaline earth metals have two valence electrons in an s orbital.","steps":["Group 2 atoms have 2 electrons in the outer s subshell.","The general configuration is ns².","For example Mg is [Ne] 3s²."],"others":"ns¹ is Group 1. ns¹np¹ and ns²np² are not the Group 2 pattern; they belong to p-block elements.","answer":"ns2"},"chem-11-05-b39":{"idea":"More valence electrons give stronger metallic bonds and so higher melting points.","steps":["Group 1 atoms give 1 electron to the metallic bond.","Group 2 atoms give 2 electrons and have smaller atoms.","So Group 2 metals have stronger bonds.","Therefore Group 1 melting points are lower, e.g. Na 98 °C and Mg 650 °C."],"others":"Melting points are not identical. Group 1 is not higher. Melting point is related to the number of valence electrons, so saying it is unrelated is wrong.","answer":"Group 1 metals generally have LOWER melting points than Group 2 metals"},"chem-11-05-b40":{"idea":"Milk of magnesia is a mild base that is only slightly soluble. That is why it is safe as an antacid.","steps":["Mg(OH)₂ is a weak base with low solubility.","It neutralises stomach acid gently.","So it is sold as milk of magnesia."],"others":"Epsom salt (MgSO₄·7H₂O) is a laxative. Caustic soda (NaOH) is a strong, harmful base. LiF is not used as an antacid.","answer":"Mg(OH)2 (milk of magnesia)"},"chem-11-05-b41":{"idea":"Be²⁺ and Al³⁺ have similar polarising power, so Be and Al behave alike.","steps":["Be²⁺ has charge 2 and a very small radius.","Al³⁺ has charge 3 and a larger radius.","The charge-to-radius ratio is similar for both.","So both form covalent compounds and amphoteric oxides and hydroxides."],"others":"Their radii are not identical. Both are metals, not acidic non-metals. They do not have the same valence electrons: Be has 2 and Al has 3.","answer":"Similar charge/radius ratio (polarising power), so both form covalent, amphoteric compounds"},"chem-11-05-b42":{"idea":"Use moles and the balanced equation to find how much water is taken up.","steps":["Moles of plaster = 29 ÷ 145 = 0.2 mol.","The ratio is 2 plaster : 3 water, so water = 0.2 × 3/2 = 0.3 mol.","Mass of water = 0.3 × 18 = 5.4 g.","Check: gypsum = 0.2 × 172 = 34.4 g, and 34.4 − 29 = 5.4 g."],"answer":"5.4 g"},"chem-11-05-b43":{"idea":"Covalent compounds dissolve in organic solvents better than ionic ones.","steps":["Li⁺ is very small, so it has high charge density.","It polarises Cl⁻ strongly (Fajans rules).","So LiCl has partial covalent character.","This lets it dissolve in ethanol, unlike NaCl or KCl."],"others":"Chloride ions are not soluble only in organic solvents. LiCl is not purely ionic. Melting point alone does not explain solubility in ethanol.","answer":"Li+'s small size and high charge density give LiCl significant covalent character"},"chem-11-05-b44":{"idea":"Molar mass is the sum of atomic masses.","steps":["BaSO₄ has 1 Ba, 1 S and 4 O.","M = 137 + 32 + 4 × 16.","M = 137 + 32 + 64 = 233 g/mol."],"others":"217 comes from using only 3 O atoms. The other two numbers do not come from adding the atoms correctly.","trap":"Count all four oxygen atoms.","answer":"233 g/mol"},"chem-11-05-b45":{"idea":"Mass of product = moles × molar mass, and the mole ratio here is 1 : 1.","steps":["Moles of CaO = 100 ÷ 56 = 1.786 mol.","One CaO gives one Ca(OH)₂, so moles of Ca(OH)₂ = 1.786 mol.","Mass = 1.786 × 74 = 132.1 g.","Check: water used is 1.786 × 18 = 32.1 g, and 100 + 32.1 = 132.1 g."],"answer":"132.1 g"},"chem-11-05-b46":{"idea":"For Group 1 carbonates, a smaller cation polarises CO₃²⁻ more and so makes it less stable to heat.","steps":["Li⁺ is the smallest and most polarising, so Li₂CO₃ breaks down easily.","K⁺ is the largest, so K₂CO₃ is the most stable.","Order from least to most stable is Li₂CO₃ < Na₂CO₃ < K₂CO₃."],"others":"The two orders starting with Na₂CO₃ or K₂CO₃ as least stable reverse the trend. They are not equally stable, since Li₂CO₃ decomposes at a low temperature.","answer":"Li2CO3 < Na2CO3 < K2CO3"},"chem-11-05-b47":{"idea":"The Solvay process needs CO₂, and a cheap source is limestone.","steps":["Limestone is heated: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.","The CO₂ is passed into ammoniated brine to form NaHCO₃.","CaO is later used to recover NH₃ from NH₄Cl."],"others":"NaCl does not release CO₂. Air has too little CO₂ for industry. NH₄Cl is a by-product and gives NH₃ with CaO, not CO₂.","answer":"Limestone (CaCO3), heated to give CaO and CO2"},"chem-11-05-b48":{"idea":"Dissolving needs hydration enthalpy to beat lattice enthalpy.","steps":["Be²⁺ is tiny, so its hydration enthalpy is very large.","This beats the lattice enthalpy, so BeSO₄ dissolves.","Ba²⁺ is large, so hydration enthalpy is small.","The lattice enthalpy wins, so BaSO₄ stays insoluble."],"others":"The claim that Be has the largest lattice enthalpy gets the trend backwards. Be²⁺ is the smallest ion, not the largest. BaSO₄ is insoluble because of enthalpies, not an oxide coating.","answer":"BeSO4: tiny Be2+ has a hydration enthalpy big enough to beat the lattice enthalpy; BaSO4: large Ba2+ is weakly hydrated, so lattice enthalpy wins"},"chem-11-05-b49":{"idea":"Two properties can be unrelated. The solubility of a compound does not decide how well a metal reduces.","steps":["Solubility depends on lattice and hydration enthalpies of one compound.","Reducing power of a metal depends on its ionisation energy and electrode potential (E°).","These are different quantities, so the link made by the student is false."],"others":"Reducing strength does not depend only on atomic mass. A less soluble carbonate does not show tighter holding of electrons. The premise is not false in a way that rescues the argument.","trap":"Do not link a compound property to a metal property.","answer":"Compound solubility and the metal's reducing strength are unrelated; reducing strength depends on ionisation energy and E°"},"chem-11-05-b50":{"idea":"Bicarbonates are less stable than carbonates when heated.","steps":["HCO₃⁻ breaks down easily when heated.","2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂ happens at a low temperature.","CO₃²⁻ in Na₂CO₃ is stable up to its melting point near 850 °C."],"others":"The extra sodium argument is not a valid reason. Moisture absorption is not what decomposes NaHCO₃. A liquid carbonate can decompose; melting is not the reason.","answer":"HCO3- is less heat-stable than CO3^2-, so NaHCO3 gives Na2CO3, H2O and CO2 at a low temperature"},"chem-11-05-b25":{"idea":"Equal moles of two elements have masses in the ratio of their molar masses.","steps":["One mole of any element has a mass equal to its molar mass.","So for equal moles, mass of Ca ÷ mass of Ba = 40 ÷ 137.","40 ÷ 137 = 0.29.","Check: 2 mol each gives 80 ÷ 274, which is also 0.29."],"trap":"The ratio has no unit, since g/mol cancels.","answer":"0.29"}};
